From: OpenWrt E8450 investigation <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_ppe: clamp set_queue to the hardware field width

mtk_foe_entry_set_queue() writes its @queue argument straight into a
FIELD_PREP() call against a 4-bit (v1: MTK_FOE_IB2_QID, GENMASK(3,0),
queues 0-15) or 7-bit (v2/v3: MTK_FOE_IB2_QID_V2, GENMASK(6,0), 0-127)
hardware bitfield, with no range check anywhere in the call chain.
FIELD_PREP() silently truncates an oversized value to the field width
instead of rejecting it (e.g. queue=153 on this board's v1 4-bit field
becomes 153 & 0xF = 9) - a silent wrong-queue write, not a crash, but a
real correctness hazard for any future caller that derives @queue from
data this driver doesn't otherwise validate (this fork's own
qos_toggle==2 ct-mark-based explicit queue select in
mtk_flow_set_output_device(), mtk_ppe_offload.c, reads a raw 16-bit
slice of a userspace-writable conntrack mark as a literal queue number).

Not currently reachable in this tree: 999-ppe-93's ct mark ==
MTK_PPE_EXCEPTION_TAG (0x99) check in mtk_flow_offload_replace() runs
unconditionally before mtk_flow_set_output_device() is ever called, so
that specific value never reaches this function today. But that safety
is purely a property of call order in a different function, not
anything mtk_foe_entry_set_queue() itself enforces - a future refactor
of mtk_flow_offload_replace(), or any other call path that ever derives
a queue number from unchecked conntrack-mark data, would silently
reintroduce it. Defensive fix, independent of any AQM v2 work: clamp
@queue to the hardware field's actual representable range before
FIELD_PREP(), and WARN_ON_ONCE (fires at most once per boot) if that
clamp ever actually changes the value, so an out-of-range caller is
visible in dmesg instead of silently mis-routed.

No behavior change for any legitimate caller: every existing call site
in this tree passes a queue number already known to be in range (either
a literal QDMA queue 0-15, or DSCP-derived, or read back from a prior
mtk_foe_entry_ib2() encode/decode round trip).

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt E8450 investigation <noreply@example.invalid>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_ppe.c
@@ -634,12 +634,19 @@
 			    unsigned int queue)
 {
 	u32 *ib2 = mtk_foe_entry_ib2(eth, entry);
+	unsigned int max_queue;
 
 	if (mtk_is_netsys_v2_or_greater(eth)) {
+		max_queue = FIELD_MAX(MTK_FOE_IB2_QID_V2);
+		if (WARN_ON_ONCE(queue > max_queue))
+			queue = max_queue;
 		*ib2 &= ~MTK_FOE_IB2_QID_V2;
 		*ib2 |= FIELD_PREP(MTK_FOE_IB2_QID_V2, queue);
 		*ib2 |= MTK_FOE_IB2_PSE_QOS_V2;
 	} else {
+		max_queue = FIELD_MAX(MTK_FOE_IB2_QID);
+		if (WARN_ON_ONCE(queue > max_queue))
+			queue = max_queue;
 		*ib2 &= ~MTK_FOE_IB2_QID;
 		*ib2 |= FIELD_PREP(MTK_FOE_IB2_QID, queue);
 		*ib2 |= MTK_FOE_IB2_PSE_QOS;
